  system it becomes extraordinarily  fragile  some of these examples of fragility  include significant  income inequality when a pandemic hits  income inequality in this case became  dramatically exacerbated so when you  start with an unequal situation you have  a massive  collapse of the economy and then you get  a k-shaped recovery where the very rich  do really really well  and a lot of people do not that is an  example of something  where a more robust system of social  safety nets  among many other changes and policies  we've been talking about  health care not being linked to  employment and so many other things  living wage all these different things  they make the system less fragile to  begin with and so  i think that there's a debate to be had  as to should there be a fourth  coronavirus stimulus would there be an  appetite for something else in may  or in june or whatever the case may be  maybe they can get it done maybe they  can't  much less covet stimulus would have been  needed obviously if trump had better  handled the virus  and not as many jobs had been lost and  all of that but just as importantly  if the systemic changes i've been  talking about for a decade  were in place things wouldn't have  gotten nearly as bad as they got  if people had health care not connected  to work if social safety nets were more  robust  if everybody earned a living wage right  we we really need  change for everyone systemically  rather than i would argue another bill  that helps  some people even if it's most if the  current recovery continues  job creation reduction in unemployment  rate  reductions and deaths reductions in  hospitalizations  uh we're seeing a lot of jobs come back  as part time which hopefully will become  full time we talked about the jobs  report  from february at the beginning of the  week and um  it's you know this this third stimulus  makes sense economically and politically  joe biden  ran on it promised it many senate  democrat democratic candidates  also ran on it um it would be a major  failure not to have done it we're not at  a point where we can say we don't need  that  my hope is that in three to four months  we'll be at a point  where we'll be able to say we need very  targeted stimulus for people in  particular situations  but now it's time to fix the systems  and do the real infrastructure spending  so that the next pandemic  isn't as big as a disaster at this one  right now it's too early  virus is still causing a lot of damage a  lot of people are still  unemployed will be unemployed for the  foreseeable future we're 9 million  jobs short of where we were before the  pandemic so  you give people checks you provide a  buffer you enhance unemployment child  tax credits all these things  eventually if anything good comes from  this virus in the united states  it would be realizing how far behind we  are so many other rich countries  when it comes to social safety nets and  dampening the blow of something like  this to begin with  i'm probably being naively optimistic  given some of the people that are  representing us in the house and senate  right now  and that actually relates to the next  question we're going to talk about  one of our sponsors is monk pack  it's tough to get excited about most  granola bars  they have a lot of carbs they don't  taste that great  they don't really satisfy your cravings  but monk pack has something totally  different monk pack  keto granola bars are sweet and  delicious  with a soft and chewy texture  only 140 calories one gram of sugar  and only two to three net carbs which  make them work  perfectly for the keto or low carb  lifestyles  they come in excellent flavors like  coconut cocoa chip  honey nut blueberry almond vanilla  that's my new favorite  i love these because you can just grab  them anytime maybe it's a quick  breakfast an afternoon snack when you're  busy  forget the dry bland granola bars you've  had in the past  try out monk pack keto granola